  "state": {
    "case_id": "15",
    "case_summary": "A 40-year-old man from eastern India presented with a nonhealing ulcer on the tongue present for the last 4 months. The lesion started as a small painless mass on the right lateral aspect of the tongue, which gradually grew and subsequently ulcerated. It caused mild difficulty in eating. No history of trauma or tongue bite preceding the lesion. Patient denied consuming tobacco or alcohol regularly. Medical history includes well-controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus for 2 years with oral hypoglycemic agents. No systemic complaints. No history of high-risk behavior for HIV.\n\nSingle well-defined ulcer measuring 2 × 2 cm on the right lateral aspect of tongue with a clean base and irregular and elevated heaped-up margins. No underlying induration, friability, or bleeding on manipulation. Rest of the oral cavity was normal.\n\n- Histopathological examination (3-mm punch biopsy from edge of ulcer):  - Dense diffuse infiltrate of lymphocytes, histiocytes, epithelioid cells, giant cells, plasma cells, eosinophils, and neutrophils  - Many histiocytes and giant cells contained intracellular yeasts with a surrounding halo  - Special stains (Grocott-Gomori methenamine-silver nitrate and periodic acid–Schiff) highlighted fungal forms  - Fungal culture on Sabouraud dextrose agar was negative  - HIV-1 and HIV-2 serologic testing were negativeImages:- Figure 1A Title: Tongue ulcer- Figure 1B Title: Biopsy from the ulcer showing a mixed cell infiltrate (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×40)- Figure 1C Title: Intracellular yeast forms within the histiocytes (original magnification ×100)",
